<p class="sst-lead">Across science, the <strong>shape</strong> of a thing carries the signal: turbulent structures
in weather, cell boundaries in histology, grains in a metal, electrode particles in a battery &mdash; and the
package boundaries inside an advanced semiconductor.</p>

<div class="sst-frag">
  <p>We want a single, principled language for shape that works the same way whether the object is a
  hurricane eddy or a solder bump in an x-ray CT scan. An image yields a <em>set</em> of boundary curves,
  and that set becomes a <em>distribution</em> on a manifold:</p>
  <p class="sst-chain">
    <span>image</span> <span class="sst-arrow">&rarr;</span>
    <span>a <strong>set of boundary curves</strong></span> <span class="sst-arrow">&rarr;</span>
    <span>a <strong>distribution</strong> on a manifold \(\mathcal{M}\)</span>
  </p>
</div>

<div class="sst-frag">
  <p>This walkthrough builds that language &mdash; <strong>Separable Shape Tensors</strong> &mdash; from the
  ground up, then lets you branch into the application stories it powers.</p>
</div>
